  - [1mCookie[0m — persists and is sent to the server automatically, which matters if the backend needs the ID. Adds flags to
    get right ([38;5;153mSameSite[39m, [38;5;153mSecure[39m, expiry) and sends the value on every request whether or not you want it.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m. It satisfies "persists and works across the app" for a login session, which is the
  actual requirement, without leaving an identifier on disk after the browser closes. [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is a defensible
  choice if you want people to stay recognized across restarts — but that's a product decision about staying logged in,
  not a storage detail, and it obliges us to build a logout/clear path in the same change.

  Worth flagging: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m today is a stub that returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m without ever calling
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Whichever option you pick, the "server returns a userId" part will also be stubbed for now, and I'll
  shape the return so the real call can drop in later without changing callers.

